<handout component="day10" xml:id="day-10-lecture">
    <title>Day 10</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Ch 4.3: Logistic Regression
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <objectives>
        <title>Covered in this class</title>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Logistic regression
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Logistic Regression</title>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 42%">
            <image source="figures/Fig4-1a.png"/>
            <image source="figures/Fig4-1b.png"/>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        10,000 data points
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Individuals who defaulted are shown in orange.
                        The actual default rate is 3%, so only a fraction of
                        those who did not default are plotted.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Goal: function
                        <m>C(\text{balance}, \text{income}) = \text{default/not}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>What is classification?</title>

        <sidebyside>
            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Classification: When the response variable is
                                qualitative
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Goal: Model the probability that <m>Y</m>
                                belongs to a particular category
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        p(\texttt{balance}) = \Pr(\texttt{default} = \texttt{yes} \mid \texttt{balance})
                    </md>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <image source="figures/Fig4-1a.png" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Scatter plot of two classes (blue circles and orange pluses)
                    on income versus balance plane.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Goal for Balance data set</title>

        <sidebyside>
            <image source="figures/Fig4-1a.png" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Scatter plot of two classes (blue circles and orange pluses)
                    on income versus balance plane.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    Goal: Model the probability that <m>Y</m> belongs to a
                    particular category.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Example:
                    <m>\Pr(\texttt{default} = \texttt{yes} \mid \texttt{balance})</m>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Use notation <m>p(\texttt{balance})</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Choice of threshold depends on comfort with
                                risk.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Note that highly contrived fake data means that
                                this is largely decidable by balance, so we'll
                                just look at that.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Why not use linear regression?</title>

        <sidebyside>
            <stack>
                <p>
                    <em>Bad idea:</em>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Set <m>Y</m> to be a dummy variable taking
                                values in <m>\{1,2,3,\ldots\}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Run regression, and choose <m>k</m> based on
                                which integer value <m>\hat{y}</m> is closest
                                to.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    Example:
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        Y =
        \begin{cases}
          1 \amp \text{if stroke}\\
          2 \amp \text{if drug overdose}\\
          3 \amp \text{if epileptic seizure}
        \end{cases}
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    versus
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        Y =
        \begin{cases}
          1 \amp \text{if mild}\\
          2 \amp \text{if moderate}\\
          3 \amp \text{if severe}
        \end{cases}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <note>
            <p>
                Ordering implies something about closeness, i.e., there has to
                be a monotonic relation between categories.
            </p>
        </note>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Is linear regression OK we have only two levels?</title>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        p(\texttt{balance}) = \Pr(\texttt{default} = \texttt{yes} \mid \texttt{balance})
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        Y =
        \begin{cases}
          0 \amp \text{if not default}\\
          1 \amp \text{if default}
        \end{cases}
                    </md>
                </p>
        <sidebyside>
            <stack>


                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Fit linear regression.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Predict default if <m>\hat{y} &gt; 0.5</m>;
                                predict not default otherwise.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Could produce values outside <m>[0,1]</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Cannot interpret this as a probability.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Regression methods cannot accommodate a
                                qualitative response with more than two classes.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Does not provide meaningful estimates of
                                <m>\Pr(Y \mid X)</m>, even with two classes.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <image source="figures/Fig4-2-clipped.png" width="100%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Probability of default versus balance.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        p(\texttt{balance}) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \texttt{balance}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Approximating the probability</title>

        <sidebyside>
            <stack>
                <p>
                    <m>\Pr(\texttt{default} = \texttt{yes} \mid \texttt{balance})</m>
                </p>

                <image source="figures/Fig4-2-NoLine.png" width="100%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Probability of default versus balance.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We are trying to approximate the probability
                                with a function.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Draw some example functions.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                A linear function has issues: density at the
                                bottom overcompensates.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Values can go outside the <m>[0,1]</m> bound.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                A better approach: note 0 when balance is below
                                1000 and 1 when balance is over 2000, then add a
                                smooth curve connecting them.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Logistic function</title>

        <sidebyside>
            <stack>
                <image source="figures/LogisticFunction.png" width="100%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A plot of the logistic function.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        p(X) = \frac{e^{\beta_0 + \beta_1X}}{1 + e^{\beta_0 + \beta_1X}}
                    </md>
                </p>

            </stack>


        </sidebyside>

                <p>
                    <em>Try it out:</em>
                </p>
        <figure xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal">
            <caption>Graph of <m>p(x)\ =\ \frac{e^{b_{0}+b_{1}x}}{1+e^{b_{0}+b_{1}x}}</m>. <m>\beta_0</m> and <m>\beta_1</m> shift the curve left, right and change gain</caption>
            <interactive desmos="cw1pyzzqci" width="60%" aspect="2:3">
                <shortdescription>
                    An interactive graph of the logistic function.
                </shortdescription>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

    </paragraphs>
<paragraphs>
  <title>Logistic Regression</title>

  <p>
    <md>
      \Pr(\texttt{default} = \texttt{yes} \mid \texttt{balance})
      =
      \frac{e^{\beta_0 + \beta_1 \texttt{balance}}}
      {1 + e^{\beta_0 + \beta_1 \texttt{balance}}}
    </md>
  </p>


    <image source="figures/Fig4-2-clipped-noline.png" width="60%">
      <shortdescription>Probability of default versus balance. A sigmoid function is also shown on the same plot.</shortdescription>
    </image>


</paragraphs>

<example>
<statement>
    <p>
        What will the drawn logistic regression classifier predict for each of the following values of <c>Balance</c>.
	Choose the decision boundary to be <m>p &gt; 0.1</m>.
    </p>
    <sidebyside>
  <stack>
    <image source="figures/Fig4-2b.png" width="100%">
      <shortdescription>Probability of default versus balance. A sigmoid function is also shown on the same plot.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </stack>
  <stack>
    <tabular>
      <row>
        <cell><em>Balance</em></cell>
        <cell><em>Prediction</em></cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell>0</cell><cell></cell></row>
      <row><cell>500</cell><cell></cell></row>
      <row><cell>1000</cell><cell></cell></row>
      <row><cell>1500</cell><cell></cell></row>
      <row><cell>2000</cell><cell></cell></row>
      <row><cell>2500</cell><cell></cell></row>
    </tabular>
  </stack>
</sidebyside>
    </statement>
    <answer>        
                <tabular>
      <row>
        <cell><em>Balance</em></cell>
        <cell><em>Prediction</em></cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell>0</cell><cell>No</cell></row>
      <row><cell>500</cell><cell>No</cell></row>
      <row><cell>1000</cell><cell>No</cell></row>
      <row><cell>1500</cell><cell>No</cell></row>
      <row><cell>2000</cell><cell>Yes</cell></row>
      <row><cell>2500</cell><cell>Yes</cell></row>
    </tabular>
        
    </answer>
</example>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Odds</title>

  <p>
    <md>
      \frac{p(x)}{1-p(x)}
      = \frac{\Pr(Y = 1 \mid X=x)}{1-\Pr(Y = 1 \mid X=x)}
      = \frac{\Pr(Y = 1 \mid X=x)}{\Pr(Y = 0 \mid X=x)}
    </md>
  </p>



      <image source="figures/1024px-Probability_vs_odds.png" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>A pie chart showing the difference between probability and odds.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>The logistic function is chosen so that the log odds are linear.</p></li>
          <li><p>Odds can take any value from 0 (low odds) to <m>\infty</m> (high odds).</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>



  <example>
    <statement>
        <p>
           If the probability of default is 90%, what are the odds?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
            <m>p(x) = 0.9</m>
        </p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>\frac{0.9}{1-0.9} = 9</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
  </example>
  <example>
    <statement>
        <p>
             If the odds are <m>1/3</m>, what is the probability of default?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
           <m>\frac{p}{1-p} = 1/3</m>
        </p>
    </hint>
    <hint>
        <p>
            <m>3p = 1-p</m>
        </p>
    </hint>
    <hint>
        <p>
            <m>4p = 1</m>
        </p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
        <p>
             <m>p = 1/4</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
  </example>
</paragraphs>
<paragraphs>
  <title>Making the nonlinear linear</title>

  <sidebyside>
    <stack>
      <p>Assume the (natural) log odds (logits) follow a linear model.</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \log\left(\frac{p(x)}{1-p(x)}\right) = \beta_0 + \beta_1x
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Do some algebra and get <m>p(x)</m>:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          p(x) = \frac{e^{\beta_0 + \beta_1x}}{1 + e^{\beta_0 + \beta_1x}}
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>\frac{p(x)}{1-p(x)}</m> are the odds in favor of A. <m>\log\left(\frac{p(x)}{1-p(x)}\right)</m> measures the relative evidence between the two alternatives with probabilities <m>p(x)</m> and <m>1-p(x)</m>
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Perceptually, relative change in a stimulus matters more than absolute change. 
                    <ul>
          <li><p>Sensory perception works by times rather than additions.</p></li>
            </ul>
                </p>
            </li>          
        </ul>
      </p>
      <image source="figures/WebersLaw.png" width="60%">
        <shortdescription>A 2 by 2 tile of plots. The first column shows a scatter plot of 10 and 20 points. The second column shows a scatter plot of 110 and 120 points.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
        <url href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weber-Fechner_law_demo_-_dots.svg">Wiki image link</url>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>

