</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Interpreting the coefficients</title>

  <sidebyside>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <md>
          p(x) = \frac{e^{\beta_0 + \beta_1x}}{1 + e^{\beta_0 + \beta_1x}}
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \log\left(\frac{p(x)}{1-p(x)}\right) = \beta_0 + \beta_1x
        </md>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <image source="figures/Table4-1.png" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Coefficients, standard error, z-statistic, and p-values for a logistic regression model with balance as a predictor for defaulting on credit card balance.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><m>\beta_1</m> means increasing <m>x</m> by one unit increases the log odds by <m>\beta_1</m> units.</p></li>
          <li><p>In this case, increasing the balance by 1 increases the log odds of default by 0.0055.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Confusion Matrix: Predicting <m>\texttt{default}</m> from <m>\texttt{balance}</m></title>

  <sidebyside widths="50% 45%">
    
      <image source="figures/Table4-4.png">
        <shortdescription>Confusion matrix for predicting default on credit card from balance.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    
    
      <image source="figures/ConfusionMatrixEx.png">
        <shortdescription>General confusion matrix structure with arbitrary entries.</shortdescription>
      </image>
  </sidebyside>
    
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Overall error: <m>\frac{(c+b)}{N} = 2.75\%</m>.</p></li>
          <li component="slides-inst-notes"><p>This table is from the LDA section, but we can use it to understand the confusion matrix.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
</paragraphs>

